{
	title: 'Preserve bottom-follow intent through incidental input',
	spec: 'The web transcript follows the live bottom through every non-navigation update. Only deliberate transcript navigation away releases following; incidental wheel, keyboard, focus, pointer and touch activity does not. Content shrink and layout clamping never change reader ownership. Readers away from bottom keep their position. Explicit send, bottom navigation and tab restoration clear incompatible linked-card anchors. Keep real-browser regression coverage for harmless input at bottom, pointer-held cursor removal/clamping, new content, returning to bottom, link/send conflicts and reading/selection away. Diagnose causal state transitions using opt-in bounded structural data without transcript text; generic traffic cannot erase all scroll evidence. Absence of drift warnings is not proof of correctness. Implementation is based on reproduced browser failures, prior user reports and measured behavior; distinguish exact incident attribution from reproduced causes. Remove faulty mechanisms rather than stacking workarounds: affected non-test source must have fewer nonblank noncomment lines than the starting implementation, including diagnostics additions. Record causes, historical requirement corrections and verification limits. Web-only implementation; both clients retain reader-owned viewports.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Diagnostics retain structural scroll snapshots at causal transitions and preserve pull triggers. The 32-entry report ring reserves retention for the newest 12 scroll/pulled/drift entries under generic-event pressure, including failed-send merges; a cumulative dropped counter exposes eviction. The existing probe records a sustained unpinned gap as evidence, not a failure, and still reports pinned drift. Snapshot values are projected through shared numeric/boolean allowlists; causes are fixed labels, never text. Older hosts safely ignore added fields and skip the new scroll kind. Generic traffic and report scheduling make absent drift records insufficient evidence of correctness.',
		'Real Chromium reproduced harmless downward wheel and keyboard focus/navigation at bottom disabling following; the next appended row left the cursor below view. Removing the cursor while a pointer was held caused browser clamping that the old drag heuristic misclassified. A linked-card hold overrode explicit bottom-follow on the next mutation. Input now arms a position baseline; actual movement beyond a layout clamp changes ownership. Removed press/touch suppression and release bookkeeping. Forced following and restoration clear stale holds. Exact input behind the reported screenshots cannot be recovered from old logs.',
		'Historical audit found the no-op downward-input failure had already been reproduced but not implemented. Existing tests exercised pristine pinned growth, not harmless input followed by growth. Existing drift probe trusted pinned state and generic traffic evicted causal breadcrumbs, so silence did not establish correctness. An explicit provider-model fixture replaces obsolete prior-session metadata setup: otherwise browser streaming tests silently exercised onboarding instead of the fake stream.',
		'Browser verification exposed stale fixtures rather than new product failures: initial socket events include host-mode; command output joins its command card (yg9); expanded Bash details no longer prefix a dollar sign; completion selection follows the actual first option rather than a hard-coded command; compact picker sizing is tested by content fit, not an obsolete arbitrary pixel cap. Reader simulations target the transcript instead of dispatching global wheel events. Style-growth tests target an actual Card, not the cursor row. No timeouts were lengthened or flaky assertions retried unchanged. Historical audit covered the latest 100 sessions by creation time; only independently verified recurring web failures informed this fix. Explicit send, queue, steer, synchronous submissions, linked-card send, native touch navigation, background tab return and selection were exercised in actual Chromium; no pending-count change was justified by those passing reproductions.',
		'The retained native wheel regression exposed an additional real compositor-order race: Chromium moved scrollTop before delivering the passive wheel callback. Reading the DOM in that callback erased the pre-input baseline and pulled the reader back. The implementation now retains the last observed/programmatic top independently of whether input is armed; input never replaces that baseline. The unchanged native regression passed after this correction, and a deterministic unit regression models movement before input delivery. No timeout increase or weakened navigation assertion was used.',
		'Final focused verification: ./test-expensive src/host/web.test.ts src/web/scroll.test.ts src/host/web-diagnostics.test.ts passed 64 tests with zero failures, including unchanged native away/return coverage after the compositor correction. Full TypeScript noEmit and scoped oxlint passed. Affected non-test source measured 395 to 394 nonblank noncomment lines: scroll.ts 203 to 178, diagnostics additions net 24. Baseline includes the pre-existing linked-card hold integration; tests and task specifications are excluded. Runtime adds no polling loop: it reuses the existing 250ms opt-in probe and bounded 32-entry reporting ring. Input retains one numeric position and one intent flag; no growing gesture history.'
